LLAMA-3 PROMPT DEBUG
input_only: False
instruction: "Develop a web application using Node.js that provides users with a dashboard to manage and analyze their social media activity and engagement across multiple platforms, such as Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram. The program should allow users to view their account statistics, including follower count, engagement rate, and demographics, and provide insights into their posting strategy and content performance. The UI should be intuitive and user-friendly, with customizable dashboards and interactive charts and graphs to display data. The application must also support social media authentication and authorization, ensuring security and privacy for users' social media accounts."
target: 'To begin developing this web application using Node.js, follow these steps:\n\n1. Set up a Node.js project using your preferred framework (Express.js, Koa.js, etc.)\n2. Install necessary dependencies, such as Passport.js for social media authentication, and any chart/graph libraries you wish to use\n3. Create a database structure to store user data and social media account information\n4. Implement social media authentication using Passport.js and allow users to connect their social media accounts to the web application\n5. Fetch social media data and store it in the database using their respective APIs\n6. Design and implement the UI for the dashboard page with customizable widgets and interactive charts/graphs\n7. Create backend logic to manipulate and filter data for the dashboard display\n8. Provide user settings for customizing their dashboard and managing their connected social media accounts\n9. Ensure that the web application is secure and adheres to best practices for user authentication and authorization\n10. Test, debug, and deploy the application to a production environment\n\nBy following these steps and using the appropriate Node.js tools and best practices, you can create a robust and user-friendly web application for managing and analyzing social media activity.'
formatted_prompt: '<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nDevelop a web application using Node.js that provides users with a dashboard to manage and analyze their social media activity and engagement across multiple platforms, such as Facebook, Twitter, and Instagram. The program should allow users to view their account statistics, including follower count, engagement rate, and demographics, and provide insights into their posting strategy and content performance. The UI should be intuitive and user-friendly, with customizable dashboards and interactive charts and graphs to display data. The application must also support social media authentication and authorization, ensuring security and privacy for users\' social media accounts.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nTo begin developing this web application using Node.js, follow these steps:\n\n1. Set up a Node.js project using your preferred framework (Express.js, Koa.js, etc.)\n2. Install necessary dependencies, such as Passport.js for social media authentication, and any chart/graph libraries you wish to use\n3. Create a database structure to store user data and social media account information\n4. Implement social media authentication using Passport.js and allow users to connect their social media accounts to the web application\n5. Fetch social media data and store it in the database using their respective APIs\n6. Design and implement the UI for the dashboard page with customizable widgets and interactive charts/graphs\n7. Create backend logic to manipulate and filter data for the dashboard display\n8. Provide user settings for customizing their dashboard and managing their connected social media accounts\n9. Ensure that the web application is secure and adheres to best practices for user authentication and authorization\n10. Test, debug, and deploy the application to a production environment\n\nBy following these steps and using the appropriate Node.js tools and best practices, you can create a robust and user-friendly web application for managing and analyzing social media activity.<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nThank you for the steps, but can you give me more information about which chart/graph libraries would be suitable to use for this web application? I want to make sure I pick the best option for displaying social media data.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nSure! Here are some commonly used chart/graph libraries that can be useful for displaying social media data:\n\n1. Chart.js: This is a popular open-source charting library that provides a wide range of chart types such as line charts, bar charts, pie charts, and radar charts. It is easy to use and is highly customizable. Chart.js can be used with any JavaScript framework as well.\n\n2. D3.js: This is a powerful data visualization library that offers a range of tools for creating complex data visualizations such as tree maps, scatterplot matrices, and chord diagrams. It provides a lot of freedom for customization but it also comes with a steeper learning curve.\n\n3. Highcharts: This is a charting library that has a comprehensive range of chart types and offers many customization settings. It offers a variety of interactive features, which makes it easy to zoom in and out on specific sections of data. The library has a free version and a paid version.\n\n4. Google Charts: This is a popular and widely used charting tool developed by Google. It\'s easy to use and offers a range of chart types including bar charts, line charts, scatterplots, and more. Google Charts also offers many customization settings like colors, labels, and axis ranges.\n\n5. FusionCharts: This is a JavaScript charting library that offers a lot of flexibility in terms of design and interactivity. Its key features include over 100 chart types, thousands of data visualization templates, and real-time charting.\n\nThere are many other charting libraries available, but these are some popular ones that can be used for displaying social media data. Selecting one ultimately depends on the specific needs and requirements of the web application.<|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you provide some examples of how I can use these libraries to present social media engagement data in a visually appealing way? It would be great to see some sample code or screenshots to get a better idea of the capabilities of each library.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCertainly! Here are some examples of how you can use the chart/graph libraries to present social media engagement data. 1. Example using Chart.js:\n```\n// Code to generate a line chart with Chart.js\n\nvar ctx = document.getElementById(\'myChart\').getContext(\'2d\');\nvar myChart = new Chart(ctx, {\n    type: \'line\',\n    data: {\n        labels: [\'Jan\', \'Feb\', \'Mar\', \'Apr\', \'May\', \'Jun\', \'Jul\'],\n        datasets: [{\n            label: \'Number of Likes\',\n            data: [12, 19, 3, 5, 2, 3, 7],\n            fill: false,\n            borderColor: \'#2196f3\',\n            borderWidth: 2\n        }]\n    },\n    options: {\n        scales: {\n            yAxes: [{\n                ticks: {\n                    beginAtZero: true\n                }\n            }]\n        }\n    }\n});\n```\nThis code generates a line chart using Chart.js to display the number of likes received over several months. The chart is customizable in terms of colors, labels, axes scaling, and more. Besides the line chart type, Chart.js also offers many additional charts such as bar charts, pie charts, and more. 2. Example using D3.js:\n```\n// Code to generate a scatterplot with D3.js\n\nvar margin = {top: 20, right: 20, bottom: 30, left: 50},\n    width = 960 - margin.left - margin.right,\n    height = 500 - margin.top - margin.bottom;\n\nvar svg = d3.select("body").append("svg")\n    .attr("width", width + margin.left + margin.right)\n    .attr("height", height + margin.top + margin.bottom)\n  .append("g")\n    .attr("transform",\n          "translate(" + margin.left + "," + margin.top + ")");\n\nd3.csv("data.csv", function(data) {\n\n  // Add X axis\n  var x = d3.scaleLinear()\n    .domain([0, 10000])\n    .range([ 0, width ]);\n\n  svg.append("g")\n    .attr("transform", "translate(0," + height + ")")\n    .call(d3.axisBottom(x));\n\n  // Add Y axis\n  var y = d3.scaleLinear()\n    .domain([0, 10])\n    .range([ height, 0]);\n\n  svg.append("g")\n    .call(d3.axisLeft(y));\n\n  // Add dots\n  svg.selectAll("dot")\n    .data(data)\n    .enter()\n    .append("circle")\n      .attr("cx", function (d) { return x(d.views); } )\n      .attr("cy", function (d) { return y(d.engagement_rate); } )\n      .attr("r", 5)\n      .style("fill", "#69b3a2")\n});\n```\nThis code sample generates a scatter plot with D3.js, showing the correlation between engagement rate and views. With D3.js, you can create complex and interactive data visualizations. 3. Example using Highcharts:\n```\n// Code to generate a stacked bar chart with Highcharts\n\nHighcharts.chart(\'container\', {\n    chart: {\n        type: \'bar\'\n    },\n    title: {\n        text: \'Social media metrics\'\n    },\n    xAxis: {\n        categories: [\'Facebook\', \'Twitter\', \'Instagram\']\n    },\n    yAxis: {\n        min: 0,\n        title: {\n            text: \'Total engagement\'\n        }\n    },\n    legend: {\n        reversed: true\n    },\n    plotOptions: {\n        series: {\n            stacking: \'normal\'\n        }\n    },\n    series: [{\n        name: \'Likes\',\n        data: [5, 3, 5]\n    }, {\n        name: \'Comments\',\n        data: [3, 2, 4]\n    }, {\n        name: \'Shares\',\n        data: [2, 2, 3]\n    }]\n});\n```\nThis code sample generates a stacked bar chart with Highcharts, comparing social media metrics across three platforms. Highcharts offers a range of customization settings such as axis labels, colors, and more. These are just a few examples of how to use chart/graph libraries to present social media engagement data. While the code can be complex, the result is often a visually appealing and informative display of data, allowing users to better understand their social media metrics.'
